{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790541110,"nonce":"giCE07doH1FCTLe42W6/lZfxY4s=","title":"AOT: an imported module can be foreign, its members calling external link symbols","message":"Requested by caribou (caribou issue 7263d4a), so compiled Wren can call Haxe in a linked program. Today `import \"haxe:Bench\" for Bench` in a compiled module becomes a runtime import. In a linked caribou program nothing loads that module, so Bench is left unbound, and `Bench.add(s)` raises \"Num does not implement add(_)\".\n\nContract (library API, LLVM AOT):\n- AotForeignModule { name, classes }, AotForeignClass { name, members }, AotForeignMember { signature, is_static, symbol }, passed with the build options.\n- An import whose module name is a foreign module's binds to that module's class, as an import of a compiled module binds.\n- Each foreign class is installed at startup like a compiled class, with Object as its parent and no fields of its own. It is published under the module's name, so find_imported_var_from(class, module) finds it and a reload can import it.\n- Each member is a generated body. It calls its symbol with one NaN-boxed u64 per argument, receiver first for an instance member, and returns the u64 result. The symbol stays undefined for the final link.\n- new() is a static member like any other and returns what the symbol returns; WrenLift allocates nothing for it.\n- A symbol raises through WrenLift's API (wrenAbortFiber, or anything that goes through the VM's runtime_error), which sets has_error, last_error and the pending-error word compiled code polls. Setting has_error alone is not enough.","files":null}]}